    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Coefficient of a Term In the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function",
    "problem": "What is the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-x}$ about $x=0?$",
    "worked_solution": "The coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(0).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{1-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{6}{(1-x)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{‴}(0),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{‴}(0)=\\frac{6}{(1-0)^{4}}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(0)=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6=1.\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is $1.$",
